// NEGATIVE TEST -- a negative LEFT operand of `<<` has no value (N3-09).
//
// The count is 3 and the result would fit, so neither the count bound that owns
// tests/reject/const_shift_out_of_range.pd nor the round-trip check that owns
// tests/reject/const_shl_value_overflow.pd is what refuses this: C leaves
// `-1 << 3` undefined however small the answer is, so there is nothing for this
// compiler to compute that the C compiler is obliged to agree with. Refused
// outright, naming the value.
//
// `>>` of a negative is only IMPLEMENTATION-defined -- every target this
// compiler emits for arithmetic-shifts it, which is what Rust does here too --
// so that direction stays ACCEPTED, and no fixture pins it: the accept side
// pinned in tests/03_const_items.pd shifts non-negative values only.
//
// The left operand is written `(0 - 1)` so the negative value is produced by
// the evaluator rather than by the literal. Measured: `-1 << 3` is refused with
// the same diagnostic, so the guard is on the VALUE and not on the spelling.
const X: i64 = (0 - 1) << 3;

fn main() {
    print_int(X);
}
